## Code Blocks

Nextra uses Shiki for syntax highlighting at build time. Incorrect or missing language tags cause hydration mismatches.

### Language Tags

Always use the correct, lowercase Shiki language identifier. Never use uppercase aliases (e.g. `JS`).

| Content | Correct tag | ❌ Wrong |
|---|---|---|
| JavaScript / TypeScript | `javascript` / `typescript` | `JS`, `TS` |
| HTML markup | `html` | `javascript` for `<script>` blocks |
| Java | `java` | |
| Kotlin | `kotlin` | |
| Swift | `swift` | |
| JSON / config values | `json` | (empty / untagged) |

### Global Defaults (next.config.ts)

```typescript
const withNextra = nextra({
  defaultShowCopyCode: true,
});
```

### Per-Block Attributes

Add `showLineNumbers` to every code block. Add `filename="..."` when a clear file name exists.

**Never** leave a code fence with no language tag — use `text` as a fallback.

## Images

### Static Image Optimization

Use standard markdown image syntax:

```mdx
![Alt text describing the image](/filename.jpg)
```

- Store images in `public/` (e.g. `public/my-image.jpg` → `/my-image.jpg` in markdown)
- Do **not** import `next/image` manually in MDX for local images

### DocImage Component (Fallback)

All markdown images are routed through `components/DocImage.tsx`, registered as the `img` override in `mdx-components.tsx`.

```tsx
// mdx-components.tsx
import { DocImage } from './components/DocImage'

export function useMDXComponents(components = {}) {
  return { ...themeComponents, img: DocImage, ...components }
}
```

### Avoid Raw `<img>` Tags

Never use `<img>` directly in MDX. Always use markdown `![]()` syntax.

### JSX Tag Parsing in Prose

Wrap UI label strings that contain angle brackets in backticks:

```mdx
❌ drag the </> HTML web resource widget
✅ drag the `</>` HTML web resource widget
```

### Avoid `<p>` Tag Nesting

Never use raw `<p>` tags inside MDX content — causes hydration errors.

```mdx
❌  <p>Default - N/A</p>
    <p>Options - on, api</p>

✅  Default - N/A | Options - on, api
```

## Links

### Internal Links

Nextra auto-converts relative/absolute links to Next.js `<Link>` — no manual import needed.

```mdx
✅ [Configuration Reference](./attributes)
✅ [Browser Agent](/sdk/browseragent)
✅ [Properties](#properties-and-methods)
```

Never use raw `<a href="...">` for internal navigation — it bypasses Next.js routing and prefetching.

### External Links

Nextra auto-detects external URLs and renders `<a target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">` with `↗` icon.

**Never** override the `a` component in `mdx-components.tsx` — doing so removes built-in styles, the `↗` icon, and auto-`target="_blank"`.

### Broken Internal Links

Verify every internal path resolves to an actual MDX file. If a target page doesn't exist, delink and keep as plain prose until the page is added.

```mdx
❌ [Visitor API](/webservices/visitor_agent/visitor/)   — page doesn't exist → broken route
✅ Visitor API documentation                            — delinked until the page is added
```

## Migrating from Hugo

### Front Matter Format

Replace TOML `+++` delimiters with YAML `---` and drop Hugo-specific keys:

```mdx
❌ Hugo
+++
title = "My Page"
date = 2025-01-01
draft = false
weight = 10
noindex = true
[_build]
  list = 'never'
  render = 'always'
+++

✅ Nextra
---
title: My Page
description: "..."
---
```

Keys to **remove entirely**: `date`, `draft`, `weight`, `noindex`, `[_build]`.
`noindex` → use `display: 'hidden'` in `_meta.js` instead.

### Hugo Shortcodes

| Hugo shortcode | Nextra replacement |
|---|---|
| `{{%children ...%}}` | Nothing — child pages appear in sidebar automatically |
| `{{< figure ... >}}` | Markdown `![]()` image syntax |
| `{{< highlight ... >}}` | Fenced code block with language tag |

### Hugo Image Parameters

Strip Hugo image shortcode query params:

```mdx
❌ ![Alt](/images/screenshot.png?height=350px&classes=left)
✅ ![Alt](/screenshot.png)
```

### File Naming

```text
❌ content/section/_index.md
✅ content/section/index.mdx   (with asIndexPage: true in front matter)
```

All other `.md` files should become `.mdx`.

## Steps Component

Use `Steps` for sequential, ordered processes (installation, setup, integration flows).

```mdx
import { Steps } from 'nextra/components'

<Steps>
### Step one title

Step content — can include code blocks, callouts, and any MDX.

### Step two title

More content.
</Steps>
```

- Each `###` inside `<Steps>` becomes a numbered step automatically
- Do **not** number headings manually (`1.`, `2.`) — `Steps` handles that
- Use `Steps` instead of a plain ordered list whenever steps contain code or sub-structure

## Tabs Component

Use `Tabs` when the same concept has multiple implementations (languages, package managers, OS variants).

```mdx
import { Tabs } from 'nextra/components'

<Tabs items={['Swift', 'Objective-C']}>
  <Tabs.Tab>
  ```swift showLineNumbers
  // Swift implementation
  ```
  </Tabs.Tab>
  <Tabs.Tab>
  ```objectivec showLineNumbers
  // Obj-C implementation
  ```
  </Tabs.Tab>
</Tabs>
```

- `items` array order must match `<Tabs.Tab>` order
- Each `<Tabs.Tab>` is independent MDX — it can contain any content, not just code
- Never use `Tabs` for fewer than 2 variants

## Callout Component

```mdx
import { Callout } from 'nextra/components'

<Callout>Default style — use for general notes.</Callout>
<Callout type="info">Informational — tips, clarifications.</Callout>
<Callout type="warning">Caution — non-breaking issues, deprecations.</Callout>
<Callout type="error">Breaking or dangerous actions.</Callout>
```

- Default (`no type`) renders a neutral callout
- Use `type="warning"` for version-difference notices and deprecations
- Use `type="error"` sparingly — only for data loss or irreversible consequences

## Anchor Slugs (github-slugger)

Nextra uses [github-slugger](https://github.com/Flet/github-slugger) to convert headings into URL anchors.

**Algorithm:**
1. Lowercase all text
2. Replace every contiguous sequence of non-alphanumeric characters with a single `-`
3. Trim leading and trailing `-`

**Examples:**

| Heading | Anchor |
|---|---|
| `## Getting Started` | `#getting-started` |
| `## GlanceSDKEventCode` | `#glancesdkeventcode` |
| `### \`methodName(param:)\`` | `#methodname-param` |
| `### \`hostedSessionDidEnd(errorMessage:)\`` | `#hostedsessiondidend-errormessage` |
| `## Not working — agent sees content` | `#not-working-agent-sees-content` |

> Em dashes (`—`), parentheses, colons, backticks, and all other non-alphanumeric characters are treated as non-alphanumeric — they merge with adjacent whitespace into a single `-`.

**Always derive anchor `#` values from actual heading text.** Never guess or abbreviate.

## `_meta.js` Separators

Add visual section dividers between sidebar groups using a `type: 'separator'` entry:

```javascript
export default {
  overview: 'Overview',
  '---': {
    type: 'separator',
    title: 'Guides',
  },
  'start-session': 'Start a Session',
  'video-session': 'Video Sessions',
}
```

- The key (e.g. `'---'`) is arbitrary but must be unique within the file
- `title` is optional — omit it for a plain horizontal rule

## Heading Hierarchy

Every page MUST have exactly one explicit H1 (`#`) heading. Nextra does **not** auto-generate an H1 from front matter.

- H1 must be the first heading and appear only once
- H1 text MUST match the front matter `title` value exactly
- Headings must not skip levels

```mdx
---
title: My Page
description: Page description
---

import { Callout } from 'nextra/components'

# My Page

## Section

### Subsection
```

```mdx
## Section Without H1   ❌ missing explicit H1
# Title
### Skipped H2          ❌ skipped a level
# Title
# Second Title          ❌ duplicate H1
```
